Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 08:05:59 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 529/529] [DS4.1] Dead-row cut tests: the truncated block, the
 target's law, greedy as before

With a dead-row cut and whatever the dead rows hold (random, a draft made
certain or impossible, NaN, an infinite draft logit, noise at 1e4):

- verification's and resampling's outputs are, bit for bit, those of the same
  drafts with the drafts from each cut on as -1 placeholders;
- rejection_sample commits for each sampled request what it commits with its
  draft truncated at its cut, and for each greedy request what it committed
  with its count capped at the cut plus one;
- the committed tokens pass the chi-squared test against the target at every
  position up to each cut; the positive control, the uncut scan capped after,
  fails it with NaN dead rows (and differs from the truncated block bit for
  bit with random ones);
- a captured rejection_sample reads the cut on each replay, the sink's
  candidates treat a draw at the cut as the bonus, and the step declares the
  cut's kernels (and refuses block verification the TileLang kernels do not
  serve).
